      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Start with the roofing projects you want</h2><p>Before creating campaigns, decide which roofing services are worth advertising. Residential roof replacements, emergency repairs, commercial roof systems and metal roofing have different buyers, project values and sales processes.</p><p>Prioritize the services your company can deliver profitably. Define the locations you serve, estimator availability and the types of projects you want to attract. A roofing campaign should support your actual business capacity.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Structure Search campaigns around buyer intent</h2><p>Traditional Google Search campaigns can be organized around closely related service terms. A company offering roof replacements might separate replacement searches from repairs and commercial roofing searches.</p><p>Examples of search themes include roof replacement contractors, residential roofing estimates, commercial roofing contractors and metal roofing installation. These are illustrative terms, not verified monthly search volumes.</p><p>Match each advertisement to the service a customer needs. Someone searching for a commercial roof replacement should reach a relevant commercial roofing page rather than a generic homepage.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Use negative keywords to control wasted spend</h2><p>Not everyone searching for roofing information is looking to hire a contractor. Queries related to roofing careers, salaries, DIY tutorials, equipment and construction supplies may not produce suitable customer inquiries.</p><p>Review the actual Search terms report when available and exclude irrelevant searches. Do not blindly copy a generic negative keyword list: a term that is irrelevant to one roofing business may be valuable to another.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Match each ad to a dedicated roofing landing page</h2><p>A good roofing landing page should immediately explain the service, geographic coverage and next step. Real roofing project images, appropriate testimonials, licensing details where applicable and a clear inspection process can help visitors evaluate the company.</p><p>Ask only the questions needed to prepare a useful estimate conversation. Property location, project type, ownership and timeline may be relevant. Commercial roofing can require building details and decision-maker information.</p><p>Test every form, phone link and booking flow. A paid click has little value if the inquiry never reaches the contractor.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Understand Google&#x27;s local lead options</h2><p>Google offers multiple advertising formats, and the tools available to roofing advertisers can vary by account, eligibility, location and campaign type. Traditional Search campaigns and local pay-per-lead products are not identical.</p><p>Verify the currently available campaign options in your Google Ads account before selecting a strategy. Pay attention to lead eligibility, charging rules, available controls and how qualified lead feedback can be provided.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Connect advertising to inspection booking</h2><p>New inquiries should enter a reliable follow-up process. That might include CRM creation, assignment to an estimator, internal alerts, an inspection booking link and permitted follow-up messages.</p><p>Useful pipeline stages include New Inquiry, Contact Attempted, Qualified, Inspection Scheduled, Estimate Sent and Closed. Use consistent definitions and respect communication consent and opt-outs.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure cost per qualified opportunity</h2><p>Clicks and form submissions are useful early signals, but roofing businesses ultimately need qualified projects. Track how many leads are reachable, fit the service area, book inspections and progress to estimates.</p><p>For example, suppose a campaign spends $3,000 and generates 30 inquiries. The cost per inquiry is $100. If 15 qualify, the cost per qualified inquiry is $200. If 10 inspections are booked, the cost per booked inspection is $300. These figures are hypothetical and not industry benchmarks.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>When should roofing companies scale Google Ads?</h2><p>Scale only after verifying conversion tracking, lead quality and response capacity. Increasing spend while calls go unanswered or estimates remain unsent can create more waste.</p><p>Review performance by service, location and downstream sales outcome. A smaller campaign producing valuable replacement estimates may be more useful than a larger campaign producing inexpensive, irrelevant inquiries.</p></section>

      <section class="lf-article-section">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <h3>Are Google Ads worth it for roofers?</h3><p>They can be valuable when search demand, project economics, targeting and sales follow-up are aligned. Evaluate qualified estimates and won jobs, not clicks alone.</p>
<h3>How much should a roofing company spend on Google Ads?</h3><p>There is no universal budget. Consider local competition, project margins, expected qualification rates and the amount required to collect useful performance data.</p>
<h3>Do roofing companies need separate landing pages?</h3><p>Usually it helps to match pages to materially different services, such as residential replacements and commercial roofing, when their buyers and offers differ.</p>
      </section>

